Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> Doug Freyburger wrote:
>
>> My exception is cone of cold.  I've played a number of games where I
>> decided against moving attack spells to skilled so I could use cone of
>> cold as a beam to freeze paths in the water levels. 
>
> I don't recall to have ever used that spell myself. The spell as beam
> seems quite inefficient; similar to a wand it bridges usually just (2
> or) 3 squares it seems. The enhanced skill will produce an explosion
> covering more than a 3x3 (tests seem to create even 5x5) squares area;
> wouldn't that be generally better than the beam, even to bridge paths
> over water?

Thinking in terms of combat the centered blast does work better.  It's
wonderful to blast a big crowd of monsters where many aren't cold
resistant.

I've used repeated beam cone of cold to build a road to Medusa's island,
to get to the doors of the castles and to build a road across a swamp
level.  I tend to save wands of cold for the Medusa level for the same
reason.

Thinking of ice as a bridge I am not certain that I want a large area
where monsters can crowd around me. Walking along an ice bridge one
square wide is closer to walking in a corridor than like walking
through a large room. Corridor - There are a small number of monster
types that can phase or tunnel. Ice bridge - There is a larger number
of more powerful monster types that can swim or walk on water. But
channeling most types of monsters is what happens with both a corridor
and an ice bridge.

I first started making ice bridges with valkyrie who don't have the
option of the centered blast because they max out as basic in attack
spells.  Then when I played character classes who can get to skilled or
expert in attack spells the corridor concept seemed worth more to me
than the centered blast.  When playing a wizard I might keep attack
schools at basic until I explore below the swamp then enhance it so I
can lob explosions at the scanctum and as I run back up with the Amulet.


